---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Navbar from "../components/Navbar.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  page="code-discord-bot.html"
  title="An Archiving Bot"
  description="A Discord bot that files manga pages and fan art into a thread per title, rewritten twelve times since December 2022: what it does, how it works, and what each rewrite taught me about async Python."
  shareDescription="A Discord bot that files manga pages and fan art into a thread per title, and twelve rewrites of it."
  fonts={["plex-mono"]}
  bodyClass="project-page page-ink pk"
>
    <Navbar page="code-discord-bot.html" active="coding" />

    <!--
      A Coding entry page (docs/design-personal.md, section 05): the itch, what it does,
      how it works, twelve rewrites, what I learned. The lead visual is the entry's own
      animation from the timeline. Facts come from the bot's folder: the code of every
      version, and the archive's posts.csv and threads.csv (dates read from Discord ids).
      The code is private; no token, server or image from it appears here.
    -->
    <main>
      <header class="pk-hero">
        <div class="pk-wrap pk-hero__grid">
          <div class="pk-hero__text">
            <p class="pk-kicker">Dec 2022 to now · Discord bot · Async Python</p>
            <h1 class="pk-title">An archiving bot <em>for manga and fan art</em></h1>
            <p class="pk-sub">One Discord bot, rewritten twelve times</p>
            <p class="pk-standfirst">
              It takes a queue of manga pages and fan art, sorts each image into a thread for every
              title it belongs to, files a copy on disk and writes down every post, so a whole
              collection lives in one server. It is probably the project I have spent the most time
              on. Almost none of that time went into new features: it went into doing the same few
              things better, one rewrite at a time.
            </p>
            <dl class="pk-facts">
              <div><dt>Started</dt><dd>December 2022</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Written in</dt><dd>Python, with discord.py</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Version 12, written July 2026, waiting for a live run</dd></div>
              <div><dt>Archive</dt><dd>2,402 images in 241 threads</dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
          <figure class="pk-hero__media pk-vis">
            <canvas data-vis="discord" role="img" aria-label="Image files leave a queue one at a time and are posted into the thread for their title, whose post count goes up"></canvas>
            <figcaption>Queue in, sorted threads out</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </header>

      <nav class="pk-subnav" aria-label="On this page" data-spy>
        <div class="pk-wrap pk-subnav__row">
          <a href="#itch">The itch</a>
          <a href="#does">What it does</a>
          <a href="#works">How it works</a>
          <a href="#rewrites">Twelve rewrites</a>
          <a href="#learned">What I learned</a>
        </div>
      </nav>

      <div class="pk-wrap pk-body">
        <section id="itch" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>The itch</h2>
          <!-- WHY: what the collection was for, and why a Discord server -->
          <p>
            In 2021 I wrote a batch script to put my manga collection into folders and comic archives.
            That handled whole volumes. What it could not handle was the other half of the collection:
            single pages I wanted to keep, and fan art, each belonging to one series or several. A
            folder sorts a file into one place. I wanted every image under every title it belonged to,
            and somewhere I could scroll through them.
          </p>
          <p>
            A Discord server could do that: one thread per title, an image posted into each of its
            threads. Posting by hand would never keep up, so I wrote a bot. The first one printed every
            message it saw and did nothing else. By the end of December 2022 the real one was posting.
          </p>
          <dl class="pk-stats">
            <div><dt>2,040</dt><dd>manga pages in 170 threads, from 23 December 2022</dd></div>
            <div><dt>362</dt><dd>pieces of fan art in 71 threads, from 20 December 2022</dd></div>
            <div><dt>6 weeks</dt><dd>for the manga archive to reach its size</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="pk-caption">Counted from the bot's own records; each post's date is read from its Discord id.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="does" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>What it does</h2>
          <p>
            Images wait in a queue folder, each named with its titles: <code>0042.OC.Monogatari.webp</code>
            belongs to two. Sending <code>doit</code> in the queue channel starts the bot. For each
            file it converts the image to JPEG and squeezes it under Discord's 8 MB upload limit, gives
            it the next free number, and posts it into every thread it belongs to. Each post names the
            others it went to, so a picture can be followed from one series to the next.
          </p>
          <pre class="pk-code" role="img" aria-label="Diagram: a queued file named with two titles is converted, numbered #00042, posted into both titles' threads with a note naming the other, filed on disk in the folder for #00001 to #00100, and recorded"><b>queue/</b>  0042.OC.Monogatari.webp       order . titles . type
   │
   ├─ convert to JPEG, under 8 MB
   ├─ next free number               #00042
   ├─▶ thread "OC"                   #00042 ft. [Monogatari]
   ├─▶ thread "Monogatari"           #00042 ft. [OC]
   ├─ file it on disk                #00001 - #00100/
   └─ write it down                  every post, every thread</pre>
          <p>Two more commands look after what is already there.</p>
          <ul class="pk-list">
            <li><code>delete #00042</code> removes a picture from every thread, from the disk and from the records. Its number goes back into use.</li>
            <li><code>migrate Old.New</code> reposts a whole thread under a new title, then deletes the old one. The early versions did it by putting every image back into the queue and sending <code>doit</code> to themselves.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section id="works" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>How it works</h2>
          <p>
            A Discord bot spends most of its life waiting: for a message, for an upload to finish, for
            Discord to let it send the next one. Python's <code>asyncio</code> lets it do that waiting
            without stopping everything else. I learned it on this bot, mostly by getting it wrong.
          </p>

          <h3>A pause that never happened</h3>
          <p>
            Discord limits how fast a bot can post, so the bot rests for half a second between posts.
            That one line went wrong in two different ways.
          </p>
          <pre class="pk-code"><b>r3</b>   time.sleep(0.5)            the whole bot froze for half a second
<b>r4</b>   await asyncio.sleep(0.5)   right: only this upload waits
<b>r7</b>   sleep(0.5)                 no await, so no pause at all
<b>v9</b>   await asyncio.sleep(0.5)   right again, in June 2024</pre>
          <p>
            The first version used the ordinary sleep, which stops the whole program, so while it rested
            the bot could not hear anything. The fourth got it right. Then a rewrite in 2023 dropped the
            <code>await</code>. Without it, the line makes a pause and throws it away. Python warns about
            it, but nothing breaks, so the bot posted without resting through r7, r8 and a refactor before
            version 9 put it back.
          </p>

          <h3>Locks, and then no locks</h3>
          <p>
            The bot kept its records in two CSV files, one for threads and one for posts, and several
            uploads could try to write to them at once. A lock lets only one in at a time. Version 4 used
            the kind of lock meant for threads, not for async tasks. Version 10 had a proper shared lock,
            and beside it a few places that made a fresh lock every time they ran. A lock nobody else holds
            guards nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Version 12 stopped fighting it. The records moved into SQLite, a small database in a single
            file that handles taking turns itself. On its first run it reads the old CSV files in, so
            nothing from 2022 is lost.
          </p>

          <h3>Numbers with no gaps</h3>
          <p>
            Every image has a five-digit number, and on disk they sit in folders of a hundred. When a
            picture is deleted, its number is given to the next one instead of being left as a hole. The
            whole rule fits in a few lines:
          </p>
          <pre class="pk-code">used = <i>every picture number in the archive, in order</i>
for expected, actual in enumerate(used, start=1):
    if actual != expected:
        return expected      <b># the first gap</b>
return len(used) + 1         <b># no gaps: the next one</b></pre>
        </section>

        <section id="rewrites" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>Twelve rewrites</h2>
          <p>
            The bot has had twelve versions, and each one kept the same three commands. What changed was
            how the code was put together. Laid side by side, they read like a record of what I was
            learning at the time.
          </p>
          <ol class="pk-bars" aria-label="Lines of code in each version of the bot, not counting tests or blank lines">
            <li style="--v: 0.014">r0<span><b>10</b> prints every message it sees</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.171">r3 · Feb 2023<span><b>120</b> one file; pandas for the records</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.282">r4<span><b>198</b> delete and migrate arrive</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.295">r5<span><b>207</b> the bot becomes a class</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.245">r6<span><b>172</b> polars instead of pandas</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.355">r7 · 2023<span><b>249</b> a table of commands</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.473">r8 · Jan 2024<span><b>332</b> one class per command</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.544">r8, refactored<span><b>382</b> settings in their own file</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.467">v9 · Jun 2024<span><b>328</b> and eleven tests</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.486">r7 again · Jun 2025<span><b>341</b> an old version brought back to run</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.440">r10 · May 2026<span><b>309</b> the token out of the code</span></li>
            <li style="--v: 0.479">r11<span><b>336</b> a shorter command, a clean shutdown</span></li>
            <li class="is-peak" style="--v: 1.000">v12 · Jul 2026<span><b>702</b> cogs, SQLite, checked settings, 34 tests</span></li>
          </ol>
          <p class="pk-caption">Lines of code in each version, without tests or blank lines. Versions 1 and 2 are lost.</p>

          <div class="pk-grid pk-grid--2">
            <div class="pk-tile">
              <h3>How commands grew up</h3>
              <p>
                The first bot checked each message with a chain of <code>if</code>s. Then a table that
                matched a word to a function. Then one class per command, then a shared outline every
                command had to follow, and in version 12 discord.py's own cogs. Each step made adding a
                command less likely to break the others.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="pk-tile">
              <h3>The token in the code</h3>
              <p>
                A bot logs in with a token, a password for the bot. For its first three years mine was
                written straight into the code, in more than one version. Version 10 reads it from the
                environment instead. It is the mistake every tutorial warns about, and I made it anyway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section id="learned" class="animate-on-scroll-target">
          <h2>What I learned</h2>
          <p>
            This bot turned out far bigger than I expected. It is where I learned how a program talks to an
            online service, what it means for code to wait without stopping, and how to design a set of
            commands so a new one doesn't break the rest.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most of all it taught me that a rewrite is a way to learn. Version 12 does the same three things
            version 3 did. It is six times longer, and nearly every line it added is there because an
            earlier version got something wrong: a pause, a lock, a number, a password. What is still left
            is to run it live, and retire version 11.
          </p>
          <div class="pk-actions">
            <a class="link-arrow link-arrow--quiet" href="index.html#coding">All coding projects</a>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </main>

    <Footer />

    <script src="assets/js/main.js" is:inline></script>
    <script type="module" src="assets/js/coding/index.js" is:inline></script>
</BaseLayout>
